<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golan Heights</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Golan Heights is a basalt plateau in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hermon">Mount Hermon</a>. In antiquity it held the Aramaean kingdoms of Geshur and Maacah, and later Ghassanid camps such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabiyah">Jabiyah</a>. Part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> after 1946, most of it was occupied by Israel in 1967 and annexed in 1981, a step not recognised by most of the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golan-heights.jpg" alt="Golan Heights" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Goran_tek-en · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golan_Heights_Map-SVG.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
